# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 5 on laravel-framework, TWO-STEP PROBE, RE-QUESTION
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the order
# of scenarios/laravel-framework.yaml (the judge pairs by name).
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM ATTEMPTS 1 TO 4, AND WHY THE RUBRIC HAD TO CHANGE WITH IT
# Attempt 1 scored CALLS (plain arm 0.9375), attempt 2 scored HOLDS (0.8333), attempt 3 scored
# REACHING THROUGH AN INTERMEDIATE (0.6667), attempt 4 scored the ACQUISITION ROUTE (0.6667).
# All four landed on the same side of the window, and the last read named why: the ask
# enumerated four routes in prose and the plain arm turned each clause into one regex.
# This draft scores the CONTACT and its DIRECTION. The ticket makes four fates out of one
# surface - announce and walk away, announce and use the answer, register for later,
# interrogate the listener table - and three of the four break silently. So a place named
# without the direction of its contact is not a partial answer here, it is an unusable one:
# the maintainer cannot tell whether that line is untouched or quietly wrong after the swap.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight: coverage and direction are one question. The set
# is eighteen contacts over eighteen areas, none of them in the part of the tree the object
# lives in, and the failure this criterion exists to catch is an answer dense in that part and
# thin everywhere else - enumerating inside one search scope and mistaking it for the
# framework. It also has to catch the inverse of the first four attempts: re-listing the
# components that HOLD a handle is answering the question that has already failed twice here,
# and those components are pinned in step 1 precisely so that offering them again earns
# nothing.
#
# WHY specificity demands the line AND the routine AND the direction AND the fate: the failure
# this criterion exists to catch is a run of places named together under one shared reason
# with no location on any of them. There is a second failure specific to this contract: the
# line where a component was GIVEN its handle and the line where it makes contact are hundreds
# of lines apart, and a citation of the constructor is a citation of the wrong thing. The
# constructor is where the dependency was made possible; the contact is where it happens, and
# only the contact has a direction.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the holders explicitly: they are the free half, both arms will reach
# them, they do not score, and naming them in step 1 puts the discriminator on the far side of
# them. What step 1 does NOT ask for is the roll of that far side: on the neighbouring repo in
# this vertical, asking step 1 for the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together,
# because the plain arm wrote its covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in
# step 2.
#
# WHY uncertainty is worth its weight here: this is a famous framework and the object is its
# best-known piece of plumbing. Closed-book, the model names 43 of its 58 members
# (memorization_probe samples 0.621 / 0.586 / 0.483). Every one of those members sits in the
# non-scoring contract group, so recitation buys nothing on the discriminator - but it makes
# confident assertion cheap, and an asserted contact in a file that does not contain one is
# worse than an admitted gap.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to replace the single shared object every part
  of this framework announces things through. Today that object is made once at boot,
  registered under a short name, and handed to whatever asks. When something announces, the
  listeners run inside that same call, before the announcing routine reaches its next line;
  the announcement can hand an answer back, and the announcer can halt the run on it. Anything
  can register something to be called later, and anything can ask the object what is currently
  listening. After the change, announcing hands the work off - listeners run after the call has
  returned, nothing comes back, nothing can be halted - and the listener table is settled
  before the application serves, so registering later is too late and asking what is listening
  is a question the object will not answer. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a
  docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what
  the object is today: what it does grouped by job, where the single shared instance is made
  and under what short name it is registered, what happens in order between an announcement and
  a listener running, what can come back to the announcer and what can halt the run, what else
  can be asked of the object, and which components are given one and keep it - each located at
  file:line. Second, and this is what decides the answer: every place where a contact with that
  object actually happens, with the direction of that contact named and the fate that follows
  from it.
  The distinction the whole ticket turns on is between HOLDING a handle and MAKING CONTACT.
  Holding is written down in a constructor and is fixed by the swap; it belongs to the first
  part of the answer and naming it again in the second earns nothing. Contact is a line
  somewhere else entirely - inside a routine named after the component's own subject, doing
  that component's own job, often hundreds of lines from the constructor and behind a private
  helper of the component's own naming. Some code makes contact while holding no handle at all,
  asking the application for the object at the instant it needs it, so its file contains no
  field, no constructor parameter and nothing that looks like a dependency.
  The direction is the answer, not the address. Announce-and-walk-away survives the change
  untouched. Announce-and-use-the-answer breaks silently, because the return value stops
  meaning anything. Register-for-later has to move earlier in the boot. Interrogate-the-
  listener-table cannot be answered at all. Two contacts a single search prints side by side
  can sit on opposite sides of that split.
  Most of this layer sits in parts of the framework whose own subject has nothing to do with
  announcements. The part of the tree obviously about this object holds the object itself and
  the pieces built directly on it; an answer dense there and thin elsewhere has enumerated
  inside one search scope and mistaken it for the framework.
  Every contact must be pinned to the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, with
  the direction of the contact, with whether it survives the replacement, and with one phrase
  on what goes wrong if it does not.

steps:
- name: Map the event announcement contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the object presented as one connected picture - what it does grouped by job, where
        the single shared instance is made, the short name it is registered under, the order of
        what happens between an announcement and a listener running, what can come back to the
        announcer, what can halt the run part way, and what else can be asked of the object -
        rather than as a list of routines or a roll of places the name appears? Three of those
        matter most and they are the three the ticket turns on: that listeners run inside the
        announcing call, that an answer can come back and a run can be halted, and that the
        object can be interrogated about what is listening. An answer that locates all three
        and says why they matter has understood the ticket. The registration under a short name
        is the fourth: it is the line the swap changes and it is why so much of this framework
        can reach the object without naming it.
        The step then asks for the components that are GIVEN one and keep it. Are they named,
        each pinned to the field or the constructor that fills it, and does each carry a phrase
        on what it announces? This part is deliberately the easy part and it is asked for on
        purpose - it is the half whose dependency is written down where anyone can see it, and
        the next step is about the contacts rather than the holders - so score coverage and
        location here, not difficulty.
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: how far a contact can sit
        from the handle that made it possible, and what it looks like when code makes contact
        holding no handle at all. Score whether that distance is characterised correctly, with
        one located example. Do NOT reward an exhaustive enumeration of the contacts
        themselves: it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a better answer here
        than one accurate observation. Could the next step act from this orientation with
        targeted lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine
        or class it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the
        character offset falls. Where the answer names the construction of the shared instance,
        the short name it is registered under, the routine that runs the listeners, or a
        component's field, is the line that actually constructs, registers, invokes or declares
        located, and not just asserted from a class name or a familiar convention?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part carry one phrase on what it contributes - what the registration buys a
        caller that has only a name, what each holder actually announces, what the announcer
        can learn from announcing, what halting means for the code that asked for it - and does
        the answer connect those facts to why the four things a caller can do with this object
        have four different fates after the change, rather than restating the list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name
        or a familiar framework convention? This matters more than usual here: closed-book,
        this model can name 43 of the object's 58 members, so its surface invites confident
        recitation, and saying which parts were actually opened is worth more here than
        elsewhere. Is anything the session could not resolve named as such rather than smoothed
        over, and is the observation about distance between handle and contact offered as
        something it established rather than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the event announcement contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND DIRECTION, and the two are one question here. Is every contact listed
        separately, each with the direction it runs in - announced and walked away from,
        announced and the answer used, something registered to be called later, or the object
        interrogated about what is listening? A place named with no direction is half a row:
        without it the maintainer cannot tell whether that line is untouched by the change or
        quietly wrong after it, and that is the only decision this ticket makes.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on: holding versus making contact. A component
        that receives the object and keeps it in a field is NOT an answer here - it was pinned
        in the previous step, and an answer built out of holders and their constructors has
        answered the question that has already failed twice on this repo.
        Score whether the answer found contacts of more than one direction. The overwhelming
        majority announce and walk away; a small number do something else, and those are the
        rows the whole ticket exists for. An answer in which every row has the same direction
        has either found only the easy band or has stopped reading at the call.
        Score WHERE the answer looked. These contacts sit in parts of the framework whose own
        subject has nothing to do with announcements - and they sit far from the constructors
        that made them possible, inside routines named for the component's own job, sometimes
        behind a private helper, sometimes in code that holds no handle at all and asks the
        application for the object where it stands. An answer dense in the part of the tree
        obviously about this object, or built only out of the files whose constructors name the
        type, has stopped at the shallow edge; that is the exact failure this criterion names.
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's contacts listed individually, and is
        what was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every contact carry an exact file:line, AND the name of the routine it sits in,
        AND the direction of the contact, AND whether it survives the replacement, AND one
        phrase on the failure? All five, per row. A bare filename is not specific. A run of
        places named together under one shared reason with no location on any of them is the
        failure this criterion exists to catch, and it does not become specific because the
        reason is correct.
        Is the located line the CONTACT rather than the handle? A constructor parameter, a
        promoted property, a field declaration or a setter is where the dependency was made
        possible; the contact is the line where something is said to the object, asked of it,
        or registered with it, and only the contact has a direction. Those two lines are
        routinely hundreds of lines apart in the same file, and citing the first for the second
        is citing the wrong line, not a rounding error.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Is the phrase per row a consequence of THAT direction, rather than a restatement that
        the place uses the object? "Breaks if the event system changes" is not a justification.
        "It returns the announcement's own result, so once nothing comes back this routine
        returns nothing and its caller cannot tell", "it reads the listener table and branches
        on what it finds, which is a question the replacement will not answer", "it registers
        listeners during boot, which has to move ahead of the point the table is settled", "it
        announces at the last moment of the process, so listeners that run after the call
        returns have nowhere left to run", "it announces from a destructor, so there is no call
        site anywhere that decides when this happens" are. Does the answer say, at least in
        aggregate, which contacts survive untouched and which have to be rewritten?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer distinguish contacts it traced by reading from ones it inferred from a
        name, a familiar convention, or a memory of this framework? That distinction matters
        more than usual here: this is a famous framework and this object is its best-known
        piece of plumbing, so asserting that a component announces something because it usually
        does is not the same as having read the line that does it. The short name of this
        contract is shared with at least one unrelated dispatcher in this same framework, and a
        contact attributed to the wrong one is worse than an admitted gap - does the answer show
        it settled that where it mattered? Is coverage claimed honestly - which areas it swept
        and which it did not reach - and where a direction was ambiguous, does it say so instead
        of picking one silently?
